  "text_md": "ON TOEPLITZ OPERATORS ON H1(C+) 12\n\n4. Open questions\n\n- Question 1. Clearly, the ﬁrst question we are interested in is whether there are inner functions Θ, other than the meromorphic inner functions eiτ(·), such that the associated Toeplitz operator TΘ: HΘ1 → H1(C+) is bounded, or equivalently, for which inner functions Θ the equality\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\nHΘ1 = KΘ1 ⊕ ΘH1\n\nholds. Moreover, from Theorem 1.3 we know that, if Θ is an inner function, TΘ: HΘ1 → H1(C+) is unbounded if and only if\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile2.png>)\n\nKΘ1 ⊕ ΘH1 HΘ1 ,\n\nthat is, if and only if the quotient HΘ1 /(KΘ1 ⊕ΘH1) is non-trivial. When non-trivial, it would be interesting to describe it.\n\n- Question 2. The second question delves deeper into the subject and it is related to another interesting problem. In analogy with the holomorphic case, we introduce a subspace of the real variable Hardy space H1(R). For b ∈ BMO(R) we consider the pre-annihilator of {b}, that is,\n\n\n(4.1) Hb1(R): = φ ∈ H1(R) such that φ,b = 0 . Observe that, if b = Θ ∈ H∞(C+)\n\n⊕ (HΘ1 )|R. Following the ideas in Theorem 1.3, we reformulate the boundedness of TΘ in terms of [Θ,H]. Proposition 4.1. Let Θ be an inner function. Then, the following are equivalent:\n\nHΘ1 (R) = H1(C+)|\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile3.png>)\n\nR\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile4.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile5.png>)\n\n- (a) the Toeplitz operator TΘ: HΘ1 → H1(C+) is bounded;\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile6.png>)\n\n- (b) the commutator [Θ,H]: HΘ1 (R) → L1(R) is bounded.\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile7.png>)\n\n- (c) the multiplication operator MΘ: HΘ1 (R) → H1(R) is bounded.\n\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile8.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile9.png>)\n\nand f2 ∈ HΘ1 , by using (1.2) we have that\n\nProof. We note that for every HΘ1 (R) ∋ f = f1 + f2, with f1 ∈ H1(C+)|\n\nR\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile10.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile11.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile12.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile13.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile14.png>)\n\n[Θ,H](f1) = ΘH(f1) − H(Θf1) = Θ(−if1) + iΘf1 = 0. Moreover,\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile15.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile16.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile17.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile18.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile19.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile20.png>)\n\n[Θ,H](f2) = ΘH(f2) − H(Θf2) = −iΘf2 + iP+(Θf2) − iP−(Θf2) = −2iHΘ(f2). Consequently, [Θ,H] is bounded if and only if the Hankel operator HΘ is bounded from HΘ1 to H1(C+)|R, which is equivalent to the boundedness of TΘ: HΘ1 → H1(C+), by Theorem 1.3. Thus, (a) and (b) are equivalent.\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile21.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile22.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile23.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile24.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile25.png>)\n\nNext, suppose (a) holds. Then we have that if f ∈ HΘ1 , then (Θf)|R ∈ H1(R). Indeed, we wish to show that (Θf)|R and H((Θf)|R) ∈ L1(R). Clearly Θf ∈ L1(R), and\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile26.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile27.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile28.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile29.png>)\n\nH(Θf) = −i 2P+ − Id (Θf) = −2iTΘf + iΘf ∈ L1(R), since (a) holds. Therefore, (Θf)|R ∈ H1(R), and\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile30.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile31.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile32.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile33.png>)\n\n![](<2503.07281_pg12_images/imageFile34.png>)\n\nΘf H1(R) ≤ 2 TΘf H1(C+) + f H1(C+) ≤ C f H1(C+),\n\n",
